in angular, how do you create a component and how angular services and components are working?

In Angular, components and services work together to separate your user interface (UI) logic from your data logic.

1. How to Create a Component
The easiest and best way to create a component is using the Angular CLI. Run the following command in your project terminal:

Plaintext
ng generate component my-component
(Shortcut: ng g c my-component)

This single command automatically generates four files inside a new folder:

my-component.component.ts: The TypeScript file (handles the logic and data).

my-component.component.html: The HTML template (handles the visual layout).

my-component.component.css (or .scss): The styles (handles the design).

my-component.component.spec.ts: The testing file.

2. How Components and Services Work Together
To understand how they interact, think of them as a restaurant:

Component (The Waiter): The component is what the user sees and interacts with. It captures clicks, displays data on the screen, and manages the UI. It shouldn't do heavy lifting like fetching data from a database.

Service (The Kitchen): The service handles the heavy lifting—like making HTTP network requests, calculating business logic, or sharing data across multiple parts of the app.

The Connection: Dependency Injection (DI)
Components don't manually create services using the new keyword. Instead, Angular acts as a manager that automatically "injects" (delivers) the service into the component when it is needed.

Here is a quick look at how they look in code:

Step A: The Service (The Data Provider)
You create a service to fetch data (e.g., using ng g service data). It uses the @Injectable decorator, meaning Angular can inject it anywhere.

TypeScript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root' // Makes this service available everywhere in the app
})
export class DataService {
  getUsers() {
    return ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie'];
  }
}
Step B: The Component (The UI Controller)
The component asks for the service inside its constructor. Angular sees this request and hands the service to the component.

TypeScript
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { DataService } from './data.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-user-list',
  template: `
    <h3>User List</h3>
    <ul>
      <li *ngFor="let user of users">{{ user }}</li>
    </ul>
  `
})
export class UserListComponent implements OnInit {
  users: string[] = [];

  // 1. Angular injects the DataService here
  constructor(private dataService: DataService) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    // 2. The component uses the service to get data when it loads
    this.users = this.dataService.getUsers();
  }
}
Summary of Workflow
The User interacts with the Component (UI).

The Component calls a function in the Service to handle data tasks.

The Service does the heavy work (like talking to a server) and returns the data.

The Component receives the data and updates the HTML template for the user to see.

is there singleton concept somewhere there?

Yes, absolutely! The Singleton pattern is baked directly into how Angular handles services.

By default, when you create an Angular service, it acts as a singleton.

How Angular Implements the Singleton Pattern
When you generate a service, Angular automatically adds the @Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' }) decorator above the class:

TypeScript
@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root' // <-- This makes it a singleton
})
export class DataService {
  // Service logic goes here
}
providedIn: 'root' tells Angular's dependency injection system: "Create exactly one instance of this service for the entire application, and share it with whoever asks for it."

How It Works Across Components
If you inject DataService into three completely different components, they do not get their own separate copies of the service. Instead, they all receive a reference to the exact same, single instance in memory.

Why This Matters (The Practical Use Case)
Because it is a singleton, you can use services to share and sync data across independent components.

For example, if Component A updates a variable inside the service (like adding an item to a shopping cart), Component B will immediately see that updated variable because they are looking at the exact same data store.

Can You Turn It Off?
Yes. If you don't want a service to be a singleton, you can remove providedIn: 'root' and instead list the service inside a specific component’s providers array:

TypeScript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-child',
  providers: [DataService] // Every time this component is created, it gets a fresh instance
})
But for 90% of use cases, keeping services as application-wide singletons is the preferred and standard way to manage state and fetch data in Angular.

what is angular, precise definition?

Angular is an open-source, TypeScript-based component-driven framework developed by Google for building scalable, single-page web applications (SPAs).

To be precise, it is considered a full-fledged platform rather than just a UI library because it provides a complete ecosystem out of the box, including:

